WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
This book changed the way I make

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.920
decisions. It didn't come from a CEO or

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.600
a business school professor or a

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:12.560
billionaire investor. It came from Annie

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.680
Duke, a world-class poker player who

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.760
spent 20 years at the table and won

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.720
millions. Now, poker is interesting

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.080
because that's where you can make a

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.960
right decision and still lose money. You

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.640
can make a terrible decision and still

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.920
be rewarded. So, you never know for sure

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:31.640
whether you're smart or lucky or just

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.280
emotional. That's why thinking in bets

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.960
matters. Because your career, your

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.720
money, your relationships, your biggest

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.880
life choices work the same way. But,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.360
this video is not just a book summary.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:46.760
I'm going to turn Duke's ideas into a

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.560
practical system for making better

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.200
decisions when you don't have all the

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.760
information. How to avoid emotional

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.200
pitfalls, how to separate skills from

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.360
luck, and I'll share a framework called

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.520
Ava that can help you when you can't see

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.920
around the corner. This might save you

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:05.000
years of heartache. So, let's dive in.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.520
The most counterintuitive insight we

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.240
miss is this:

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.280
Good results are bad teachers. The most

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.600
dangerous teacher in your life is

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.240
accidental success. Let's say someone

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.880
gets really drunk and it's late at night

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.680
and he decides to drive home instead of

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.640
calling an Uber. He might speed through

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.640
three red lights at 2:00 a.m. and still

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.120
gets home safely. Now, that's a good

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.000
outcome. It's a great outcome. But, it

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.640
was still a terrible decision. When

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.160
something works out in your favor in

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.520
spite of you, your brain assumes that

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.200
you made a smart bet. But, the biggest

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.040
mistake here is that we judge the

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.840
quality of the decision by the result it

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.600
produces. But, they may not be

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.720
connected. That drunk driver will

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.120
convince himself that nothing happens

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.280
when you drive under the influence of

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.281
alcohol.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.200
And that could prove fatal to him and

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:59.640
[music] others. And this is one of the

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.520
central ideas of the book. In poker, you

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.760
can play the right bet and still lose

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:08.000
the hand. And you can make a terrible

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.200
bet and still win. That gap is where

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.880
people get confused about their own

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.080
judgment. This happens in business, in

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.760
our careers, on Wall Street, in

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.440
relationships, everywhere. There are

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.200
traders who I know who believe they have

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.600
a magic touch and their trades never go

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.600
the wrong way.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.680
Until, of course, they do and they lose

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:31.560
their shirt. And same for founders and

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.840
CEOs. I was the CEO of an AI company and

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.440
we became the 20th fastest growing tech

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.080
company in the US.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.440
It was that because I was a visionary

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.320
CEO. I wish. We were the right company

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.160
solving the right problem at the right

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:48.400
time. And it worked out.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.560
Five years before and five years after,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.200
the same company wouldn't have worked.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.000
The tide is way more important than

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.000
who's rowing the boat. Our decisions

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.400
might be clean, but outcomes are rarely

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.440
clean. They're usually a mix of skills

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.360
and luck and timing and emotions. So,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:10.000
that should give us humility. And it

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.720
should help us watch out for our

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.440
self-serving bias. When we win, we think

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.000
it's because of our skills. When we

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:20.000
lose, we blame our luck. But when others

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.840
win, we say, "Ah, they got lucky."

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.362
And when they lose, we say, "Oh, well,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.440
they didn't have enough skills."

00:03:24.362 --> 00:03:29.120
>> [laughter]

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.400
>> So, the results may come back amazing or

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.800
awful.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:36.080
It does not make you a genius or a

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.280
victim. The only useful move

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.760
is to pull the result apart and ask,

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.000
"What part was skill? What part was

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.280
luck? And what can I do better next

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.597
time?" These questions sound pretty

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.400
simple, but they're so hard to put in

00:03:48.597 --> 00:03:53.720
[music] practice. So, let's first figure

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.120
out how our skills and luck get tangled

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.600
up when we make decisions, that's where

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.600
we'll find our first tool for

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.200
decision-making. That's where we go

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.080
next. A few years ago, I had a choice

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:08.200
between two paths. One was a CEO role at

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.360
a very, very cool tech company. It was a

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:13.720
startup, we'll call it company A. They

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:17.239
were about 150 people growing fast. I

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:20.040
had been at the CEO chair before, so it

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:22.240
felt right. The board and I had many

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:24.520
conversations. The chairman already had

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.560
sent me the offer with compensation and

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.320
equity, all of that stuff. And then,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.720
just before the day I was about to sign,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.480
another option showed up. It was a

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:36.480
C-level role at a company B. Now, that

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.440
company was a public company, much

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.760
bigger with a very strong market

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.760
position, and already worth about $10

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:46.320
million and counting in market cap. And

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.440
I loved the CEO.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.880
I loved the mission. So, I went through

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:55.080
my decision framework carefully and

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.680
thoughtfully, and I ended up choosing

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:01.600
company B.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:04.200
In the next few years, company A

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.520
surprisingly faced tremendous growth

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:08.760
challenges. It eventually had to shut

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.760
down. But company B's stock went through

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:14.360
the roof, up by more than 500% in

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.280
retrospect. The decision I made looks

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.600
like a brilliant move, right? But here's

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.280
the twist.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.520
A few years later, uh I had already

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.960
moved on from the company and I had

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.000
retired, but the industry dynamics

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.040
changed so suddenly that the company's

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.960
stock plummeted. And it wiped out almost

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.720
90% of its value. I still had stocks in

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.720
there. I should have sold the remaining

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:41.040
positions, but I didn't.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:43.760
And it cost me a fortune. And this is

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.200
the heart of what Annie Duke teaches in

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.640
Thinking in Bets. You can play a hand

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:50.920
with perfect skills and get lucky and

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.880
still lose. We have to get ruthless

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.400
about separating what is skill

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:58.080
and what is luck.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.360
So, let's unpack my decision process the

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.440
way she would. The first decision to

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.280
choose company B was a skill. I

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.560
suppressed my ego, I didn't get seduced

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.600
by the CEO role, and made the right

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.520
choice.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.960
But that rise in stock price that

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.360
followed, that was pure luck. And

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.680
finally,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.880
not selling the stock at the right time

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.320
was just my dumb mistake. It was my

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:25.400
greed and my emotional attachment to the

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.640
company. And literally, my delusion that

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.360
it will come back. It wasn't some bad

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.520
luck. It was just dumb mistake. So, this

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.680
has happened to me many times, and it

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:37.280
will happen to me again. And if you are

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.800
ambitious enough to play the high-stakes

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:41.520
games, it will happen to you, too.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:45.200
So, here's the tool.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.080
Don't ask, "Was I skilled or lucky?"

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.080
Because [music]

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.400
you're most likely both. And also,

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.640
because your self-serving bias has

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.520
already rigged that answer. Picture

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:58.320
someone else making the same exact move.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:02.000
And then, put them on a dial from pure

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:04.120
luck to pure skill. 90/10, 50/50, that's

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.240
the most honest number you can come up

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:09.280
with. Your job is not to look back and

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.400
beat yourself over the luck you couldn't

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.160
control or the skills you had or you

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:16.234
didn't have. Your job is to audit the

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.680
process, learn the lesson,

00:07:16.234 --> 00:07:18.760
>> [music]

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.040
>> and get ready for the next bet. And more

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:23.400
importantly,

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:25.360
know when to fold. That was my mistake.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.400
I didn't get out on time. And by the

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.560
way, if you like reading more about

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:33.240
tools and frameworks like these, you can

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:36.280
subscribe to my newsletter, as well. I

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.280
write it every Tuesday. One insight, one

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:41.120
tool, one practice. The link is below,

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.880
and it's totally free. The key takeaway

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.055
is this: Sometimes, the same decision

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:45.280
can be smart

00:07:45.055 --> 00:07:48.800
>> [music]

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:51.440
>> or lucky or foolish at different points

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.320
in your life story. That's life. So now

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.920
some of you may say, "Well, then you

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.920
have to wait until you have all the

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.800
information. So then you'll know which

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:03.120
one is which."

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:06.080
Well, that's a fallacy and we'll see why

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:09.080
next. We keep waiting to feel certain

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.640
before making big decisions and that's

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:14.720
why we wait forever. So in Thinking in

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:17.560
Bets, Annie Duke makes a simple point.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:20.280
Every decision is a bet against the

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:23.280
future. You're choosing between multiple

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:26.040
futures you cannot fully see. A job, a

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.400
house, a partner, a company. You're not

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:30.600
supposed to know everything. If you knew

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.000
everything, it wouldn't be called a bet.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:35.200
You never have perfect information.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:38.159
Also, having more information doesn't

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.159
always help. A Columbia researcher named

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:42.520
Sheena Iyengar found something very

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:46.200
interesting in a grocery store. She set

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:48.040
out a table with six jams to taste. Next

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:51.120
weekend, she came back to the same

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:54.080
station, set up the same table, but put

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.400
out 24 jams for people to taste. The

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.400
bigger display and more options

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.960
attracted more people to the table, but

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.520
when it actually came time to buy, the

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:07.880
people who saw six jams bought more than

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:10.040
the people who saw 24. More information

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.200
and more options didn't give them more

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:14.960
certainty to buy the product. It just

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:18.520
made the decision harder. Sometimes more

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:21.560
information can feel safer, but most of

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:24.040
the time it's just causing analysis

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:26.000
paralysis. It's just another excuse not

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:28.839
to move forward. And I think this is the

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:32.040
key difference between chess and poker.

00:09:28.839 --> 00:09:34.160
Chess is hard, but the entire board is

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:36.160
clearly visible. You can see every

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:38.680
piece, you can see the current

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.800
positions, but when it comes to poker,

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.960
the truth is hidden. You don't know the

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.240
other person's cards, you don't know

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.840
what card will come next, and you don't

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:51.600
know when someone is bluffing, and that

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.480
is way more closer to our lives. Because

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.800
in life, you never get to see the full

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.720
board. So, here's one practical move I

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:02.120
use. I make a two-column decision table.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:04.720
In the first column, I write all the

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:06.600
known facts. What is actually visible?

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.240
What do I know? And in the second

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:11.960
column, all the hidden cards. What is

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:14.560
unknown? What am I assuming? What I'm

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:17.640
hoping to be true. Once you separate

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:20.400
what is known from what is unknown, your

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.200
decision gets cleaner. Now, even if you

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.800
don't have all the information, you

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.440
still have to make the bet, and ask what

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:29.480
beliefs you're going to be betting on.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:32.560
That's where we go next. There is a

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:34.840
famous study called They Saw a Game.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.520
There are two universities, Dartmouth

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:38.920
and Princeton. They're both in Northeast

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.000
America. Researchers had students from

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:43.120
both universities watch the same

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.720
football game. Their own universities

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.960
were playing against each other. Same

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:51.760
place, same penalties, yet Dartmouth

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:54.000
fans swore Princeton played dirty.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.800
And Princeton fans saw the exact

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.200
opposite. They saw the game through the

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.680
team they already believed in. That's

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:03.520
why some beliefs can start distorting

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.680
what you see. That is also one of the

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:09.040
core ideas in Thinking in [music] Bets,

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:10.072
that every decision is a bet built on

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.720
what you believe.

00:11:10.072 --> 00:11:13.240
>> [music]

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:16.280
>> This is why fake news works, really.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:19.320
It feeds into what we already want to

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:21.520
believe. For example, for years, people

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:23.560
have clung to this headline, that red

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.440
wine is good for your heart. Why?

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:27.080
Because people like drinking red wine

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.640
when they come home, and they want to

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:32.000
relax in the evening. Research has since

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.600
debunked that myth clearly, many, many

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.560
times over. But, the belief has stuck

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:40.920
around because it tells us what we want

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:42.880
to hear. What's the action? Use these

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:46.098
two moves. Number one,

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.320
replace your binary thinking with

00:11:46.098 --> 00:11:50.720
[music] probabilities. Instead of saying

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:55.560
right or wrong or good or bad decision,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:58.840
ask, "How confident am I? Am I 70% sure

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:00.440
or 80% sure?" And number two, ask the

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:02.840
key question to yourself, "You want to

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.080
bet?" That's a great question. It's from

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:05.920
the book.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:07.000
When you feel certain about some

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:11.120
beliefs,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:13.320
imagine putting $100 or $1,000

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:16.440
on that belief. It will force you to

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:19.400
examine and define exactly what you

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:22.560
believe in and based on what evidence.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:24.720
So, it's better to start asking

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.480
why you believe it. Okay, so we've

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:30.160
thought about our luck, our skills, our

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:31.920
beliefs, and now we've come to a point

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:33.360
where we're ready to make a decision,

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.960
and we're going to need a framework for

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:39.040
it. That's where we go next. In the

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:41.600
Odyssey, the protagonist knew about the

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:45.280
siren song. He knew it would hijack his

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:47.280
mind. Sailors who had heard it before

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:51.280
had lost control and crashed into the

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:53.839
rocks. So, before he heard those songs,

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.640
while his head was still clear,

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:59.000
he asked his crew

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:01.480
to tie him to a ship's mast and ignore

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.880
anything he said once the song began.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:06.880
Today, psychologists call it the Ulysses

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:09.440
contract, a rule your present self makes

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:11.760
to protect you from your future self.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:14.040
The book gives us the same type of

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.400
principle. But, we have to go beyond the

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.560
book now and build our own practical

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:20.000
framework to put that advice into

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:22.520
action. This is where I use a

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.880
decision-making framework I call EVA,

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:28.279
expected value analysis. Emotions can

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:30.840
have a seat at the table, sure, but

00:13:28.279 --> 00:13:33.640
[music] Ava gets the first vote. It's a

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:36.400
four-step process. First, before making

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:38.520
any decision, map out the scenarios. Put

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:41.240
four or five branches on paper. Great

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:44.040
success, moderate success, minor

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:47.520
failure, a catastrophic failure. Once

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:50.280
these possibilities are on paper, the

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:53.040
fog starts to clear. [music] Second,

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:55.760
assign payoffs to each of these options.

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:57.640
Put a dollar figure on each branch. How

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.440
much will your gain be if it's a

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:02.000
success? How much will you lose if it's

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:05.200
a failure? And by the way, you don't

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.269
have to value your payoffs in terms of

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:06.320
hard cash.

00:14:06.269 --> 00:14:08.840
>> [music]

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:12.160
>> Include whatever matters to you. Your

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:14.240
time, your reputation, your health,

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:16.920
impact [music] on your family, anything.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:19.800
And third, assign the odds. Give each

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:21.880
branch a probability score. What is the

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.600
likelihood of that outcome happening? Do

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:25.920
it based on the information you have

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:28.320
today. And the fourth and final step is

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:30.680
to calculate the expected value, which

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:33.200
is to multiply the payoff by the

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:35.320
probability. The evaluation process is

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.240
more important than the actual numbers,

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.720
because you can stress test all the

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:41.160
scenarios, look at the worst-case

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:43.880
scenario, figure out once you put the

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:45.960
numbers down, imagine this 12 months out

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.880
from now and everything has failed

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:50.040
completely, you can write down your

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:53.160
version of how it failed and what

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:56.050
happened. When you script that detailed

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:56.440
disaster story before it has happened,

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:59.640
>> [music]

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:02.600
>> your brain builds the exit ramps before

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.200
the crash ever occurs. That is the point

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:06.600
[music] of Ava. It doesn't make your

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:09.640
decision certain, it helps you

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:12.360
understand why it's uncertain. And it

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:14.720
helps you manage the most dangerous side

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:17.160
effect of your decision.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:19.800
And that's where we go next. In poker,

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:23.640
there is a condition called tilt.

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:26.600
It's the exact moment your last hand

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:29.520
completely infects your next move. You

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:32.520
play a hand correctly, lose anyway,

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:36.040
and your anger, and your fear, and your

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:37.800
frustration, and your wounded pride,

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:40.520
all of them take over.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:43.360
Or the exact opposite might happen, too.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:45.680
You win so big, and you start betting

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:47.840
like you're invincible, and both

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:49.880
extremes are illusions. This is one of

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.640
the book's key messages, and here's

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:53.280
where the biggest trap is. When you

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:56.240
start tilting,

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:59.320
it is obvious to everyone else, but is

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:02.206
invisible to you. Because tilt serves

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:03.680
you a very toxic self-punishment.

00:16:02.206 --> 00:16:04.920
[music]

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.720
When you

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:10.280
fail, you start thinking,

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:12.240
"Why do I always screw things up?" And

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:14.480
you're locked into that world. You can't

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:17.080
see outside of it, and that is one of

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:18.240
the biggest enemies of making great

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.520
bets.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:22.480
Regret.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:24.880
That's what keeps the last hand alive

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:27.560
inside the next one. But regret after a

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.200
loss is part and parcel of how we all

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:31.839
feel as human beings, right? There is

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:34.839
nothing you can do to change what was

00:16:31.839 --> 00:16:36.160
gone, what is lost. But regret should

00:16:34.839 --> 00:16:37.440
heal,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.440
not harm.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.240
If you lose money, it doesn't mean you

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:43.839
get out of the market just when it's

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.440
recovering.

00:16:43.839 --> 00:16:46.746
I've done that.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:46.839
If you get fired,

00:16:46.746 --> 00:16:49.640
>> [music]

00:16:46.839 --> 00:16:51.440
>> it doesn't mean you stop applying to the

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.440
next amazing job.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:55.800
I've done that, too.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:57.839
If someone breaks your heart,

00:16:55.800 --> 00:17:00.040
it doesn't mean you stop believing you

00:16:57.839 --> 00:17:02.160
could be loved again.

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:05.000
This happens to all of us.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:08.280
But this is where we have to go beyond

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:10.839
the book. The real lesson is not about

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:14.680
card games. It's about realizing that

00:17:10.839 --> 00:17:16.800
outcomes can lie to you, your beliefs

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:20.360
can distort [music] you,

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:23.400
and your past regrets can paralyze you.

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:24.520
All of it is inevitable. It's going to

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.360
happen.

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:29.800
But a regret is only wasted if it

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:32.680
doesn't improve your next bet.

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:35.240
One of my favorite songwriters, John

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:36.360
Prine, has a song with this phrase I

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:40.080
love,

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:43.360
the blind spot of regret.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:44.920
Because the past follows you everywhere

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:48.200
you go.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:49.800
We hang it like a relic on our rearview

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.960
mirror,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.960
and

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.920
we keep glancing at it while we're

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:58.000
trying to drive forward.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:59.520
But the rearview mirror can't steer the

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:01.840
car.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:03.680
You can hang your regrets where you can

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:05.720
see them,

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:07.400
and then drive forward.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:10.280
Take the new turn.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:11.840
Make the new bet.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:16.960
The road ahead

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:18.920
only cares about how lucky you can get.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.120
I'll see you next week.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:22.840
Thank you.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:22.840
And I love you.

